-## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the KNN search. For a fixed window size and $k$, it is $O(N \cdot k)$ where $N$ is the number of pixels.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store depth maps.
-*   **Strengths:** Enhances overall scene reconstruction quality by clarifying depth information for both neighboring and distant pixels [S1]. Effective in sparse-view scenarios where structural loss is common [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the external depth model (e.g., DepthAnythingV2) [S1]. Performance may degrade if the prior depth map contains significant errors or misalignments.
-*   **When to use:** Sparse-view 3D reconstruction where structural continuity is critical and external depth priors are available [S1].
-*   **When not to use:** When high-quality multi-view data is available, or when external depth priors are unreliable.
+**Strengths and Limitations:**
+*   **Strengths:** Enforces continuity constraints derived from external depth maps, accounting for minor depth variations. Helps clarify depth information, enhancing overall scene reconstruction quality, especially in sparse-view scenarios [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the accuracy of the external depth estimator. May not handle large displacements between multiple depth pixels as effectively as other methods [S1].
 
+**When to Use:**
+*   Use when external depth priors are available and the goal is to enforce local geometric consistency while preserving edges. Particularly useful in sparse-view 3D reconstruction [S1].
+
+**When Not to Use:**
+*   Do not use when external depth maps are unreliable or when the scene contains large, discontinuous depth changes that the boundary value $m_{ar'}$ cannot accommodate.
 ## Background
-Depth Structural Consistency Regularization was introduced in the context of DASNeRF, which focuses on depth consistency optimization, adaptive sampling, and hierarchical structural fusion for sparse-view neural radiance fields [S1]. The method leverages modern depth estimation models like DepthAnythingV2 to provide robust depth priors, standardizing depth regularization and improving reconstruction precision [S1].
+**Origin:**
+*   The method was introduced in DASNeRF: depth consistency optimization, adaptive sampling, and hierarchical structural fusion for sparse view neural radiance fields [S1].
+
+**History and Development:**
+*   The method leverages recent depth models like DepthAnythingV2 to standardize depth regularization [S1].
+
+**Where it is Used:**
+*   Used in sparse-view 3D reconstruction to enhance the quality of scene reconstruction by clarifying depth information [S1].
 
